sec.p:
To evaluate the capacity of the synthesized magnesium silicate zeolite (Mg.ZA) for the removal of calcium (Ca2+) and magnesium (Mg2+) ions, a series of controlled batch adsorption experiments were performed. These trials aimed to investigate the influence of several operational parameters, including solution pH (ranging from 2 to 8), initial concentrations of target ions (spanning 50 to 450 mg/L), and contact durations (between 5 and 840 min). Each experiment was carried out under consistent laboratory conditions: 100 mL of the ionic solution was treated with 0.3 g/L of the Mg.ZA adsorbent at a constant temperature of 293 K. Following the completion of the adsorption process, the suspensions were subjected to filtration to recover the solid Mg.ZA material. The concentrations of residual Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions in the filtrate were then measured using inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS, Perkin Elmer). Calibration of the ICP-MS was performed using certified reference standards provided by Merck (Germany), and the accuracy of these standards was validated according to protocols set by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST). The efficiency of the adsorption process was quantitatively assessed by calculating the adsorption capacity (Qₑ, mg/g) using Eq. 1:1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
